Z||000004||hugh||10-21-2001||09:19 AM||||I've used reverse direction drill bits with some success.I usually have to drill through the bolt with progressively larger drill bits till I get to the threads and then with a small hammer and sharp punch work out the remaining piece of bolt(if the hole isn't too deep).Easy outs sometimes work,but not if you break a pipe fitting,as they may expand the fitting and make it tighter.With pipe fittings(I am talking about the small ones,like 1/8 up to about 3/8).I run a tap into the hole in the fitting and run in a bolt with a jam nut on it,tighten the jam nut and back out the fitting.||||reg||14
Z||000005||Cooter||10-21-2001||11:30 AM||||Whenever the broken bolt is higher than the piece that it is screwed into, I have used the wire welder to weld a nut over the broken bolt and remove it. I have never tried anything like they are showing- that is pretty cool!||||reg||1
Z||000006||maryjane||10-21-2001||01:22 PM||||Found the link by accident, looking for something else. My bolts never seem to break off above the surface, always down in the bore. Never tried the linked method, but I've got a large bore diesel head at work that's junk. It's got a 1/2" exh bolt broken off in it. If I can find some of those rods, I'll try it and post back. Not sure my eye sight is good enough anymore to do it without striking the side of the threads first. I used to be able to weld up everything but the crack of dawn, now half blind. Bifocals make double images of the arc and molten weld puddle.   ||||reg||1
